WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:09.084
A quadratic function has a squared term with nonzero coefficient, so its graph bends instead of maintaining a constant rate

00:00:09.084 --> 00:00:10.821
This parabola opens upward

00:00:10.821 --> 00:00:16.900
We will rewrite the same function to reveal different features without changing its outputs.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:20.691
The factored form makes zeros visible

00:00:20.691 --> 00:00:27.333
Setting the output equal to zero means one factor must vanish, giving inputs one and three

00:00:27.333 --> 00:00:31.900
Those roots locate the two horizontal intercepts of the same parabola.

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:36.086
Completing the square reveals the turning point

00:00:36.086 --> 00:00:44.744
Squaring x minus two creates x squared minus four x plus four, so subtract one to recover the original constant three

00:00:44.744 --> 00:00:48.867
The minimum occurs at input two with output negative one.

00:00:50.167 --> 00:00:53.149
A different target output can produce different solutions

00:00:53.149 --> 00:01:00.605
To reach height three, the square must equal four, so x minus two can be positive two or negative two

00:01:00.605 --> 00:01:04.333
Keep both signs when reversing a square in an equation.
